        "text": "Now I understand the auth. The `PAIR_OPEN_PATHS` set contains `/api/v1/pair/info` and `/api/v1/pair/redeem`. The new two-step endpoints need to be careful:\n\n- `begin` — computer-side (authenticated). The computer is already on the desktop with a local cookie/AGENT_TOKEN. Actually, who calls `begin`? The computer端 generates the QR code. So `begin` should be authenticated (the computer is already trusted). Actually, hmm. Let me think.\n\nActually, re-reading the task: \"第一段：`POST /api/v1/pairing/begin` → 返回 `{ code, expiresAt, confirmToken }`（`code` 是给手机扫的短码，`confirmToken` 是给**电脑端**确认用的，**不随二维码下发**）\"\n\nSo the response contains both `code` (for QR) and `confirmToken` (for the computer to confirm). The computer端 calls `begin` — this is the desktop setting page. The computer is authenticated (local cookie). So `begin` should require auth (it's on `/api/v1/*` which is authenticated by default, NOT in PAIR_OPEN_PATHS). Good.\n\nThe QR code contains only `code`. The `confirmToken` is shown to the computer user (typed in). Wait — \"必须有人在电脑端拿到 confirmToken 并输进去\". So the flow is:\n1. Computer calls `begin` → gets `code` + `confirmToken`. The QR shows `code`. The `confirmToken` is displayed to the human on the computer.\n2. Phone scans the QR → gets `code`. Phone sends the `code` to... hmm. Actually the phone needs to do something. \n\nLet me think again about the security semantics: \"只扫码（拿到 code）不能完成配对——必须有人在电脑端拿到 confirmToken 并输进去\". \n\nSo the pairing `confirm` endpoint takes `{ code, confirmToken }`. Both must be present. The phone only has `code` (from QR). The computer has `confirmToken` (from begin). So to complete, someone on the computer must input the `confirmToken`. \n\nHmm, but who calls `confirm`? If the phone calls `/api/v1/pairing/confirm` with `{code, confirmToken}`, the phone doesn't have confirmToken. So the phone can't. The computer must call it with both. But then how does the phone get the deviceToken?\n\nActually, let me re-read: \"第二段：`POST /api/v1/pairing/confirm` body `{ code, confirmToken }` → 才真正发设备令牌.\"\n\nI think the design is: the `confirm` endpoint is where the deviceToken is generated and returned. The key security property is that BOTH `code` and `confirmToken` are required. The phone has `code`; an attacker who just scanned/intercepted the QR only has `code` and cannot complete. The `confirmToken` must be supplied by someone on the computer.\n\nBut practically — how does the deviceToken get to the phone? This is a server-side interface task; \"手机端接不接是它的事\". So I don't need to fully design the phone flow. The interface just needs to:\n- `begin`: return `code` + `confirmToken` (confirmToken NOT in QR)\n- `confirm`: require both `code` + `confirmToken`, only then issue the device token.\n\nThe critical security semantics: scanning alone (having `code`) cannot complete pairing. The confirm endpoint rejects requests missing/wrong `confirmToken`.\n\nSo `confirm` must be a NO-AUTH (open) endpoint? Let me think. If `confirm` requires auth, then the phone (no credentials yet) can't call it. But actually the whole point is the person on the computer confirms. Hmm.\n\nActually, let's reconsider. The `redeem` (single-stage) is免鉴权 (open) — because the phone has no credentials before pairing. For two-step, `confirm` is the endpoint that finally issues the device token. So `confirm` must also be open (免鉴权) so that the resulting device token can be delivered. But it requires the `confirmToken` which only the computer has.\n\nWait, but if `confirm` is open and requires `confirmToken`, then the security is: an attacker with just the `code` (from QR) cannot complete — they need the `confirmToken` too. That's the \"phone never confirms on its own\" semantic. The `confirmToken` is a secret that stays on the computer.\n\nBut then, who actually calls `confirm` and how does deviceToken reach the phone? Let me think about a sensible flow:\n1. Computer (authenticated) calls `begin` → `{code, expiresAt, confirmToken}`. Generates QR from `code` only. Displays `confirmToken` to the human on screen.\n2. Phone scans QR → has `code`. \n3. The human on the computer sees the phone has scanned and types the `confirmToken` (or the phone's scan triggers something).\n4. Hmm.\n\nActually maybe the realistic flow: the person on the computer reads the `confirmToken` from the screen and types it into... no. Let me reconsider — maybe the phone sends `code` to a \"pending\" endpoint, and the computer confirms.\n\nActually, I think I'm overcomplicating. The task is explicit about the interface shape. Let me just implement exactly what's asked:\n\n- `POST /api/v1/pairing/begin` → `{ code, expiresAt, confirmToken }`\n  - Only `code` goes in QR (not confirmToken)\n  - `confirmToken` bound to code, one-time, 5-min expiry\n- `POST /api/v1/pairing/confirm` body `{ code, confirmToken }` → issues device token\n  - Requires both code and confirmToken to match\n  - \"尝试次数计入既有 attempts 限流\" (attempts count into existing rate limiting)\n\nThe key security test: \"只有 code 不能完成\" (only code can't complete), \"confirmToken 错 → 拒绝\" (wrong confirmToken rejected), \"过期 → 拒绝\" (expired rejected), \"一次性（重放失败）\" (one-time, replay fails), \"开关回滚到单段\" (toggle back to single-stage).\n\nSo I'll design the store to support a two-step flow. Let me think about the store design.\n\nCurrent `issueCode()` creates a single `StoredCode` with `hash`, `expiresAt`, `attempts`, `usedAt`. And `redeem()` checks the code and issues the device.\n\nFor two-step, I need to add a `confirmToken` concept. The `confirmToken` is:\n- one-time\n- 5-min expiry (could be same as code TTL, 300s)\n- bound to the code\n- stored hashed (sha256, per \"密钥不进 git/日志/客户端... 配对码与设备令牌只存 sha256\")\n\nLet me think about how `begin` and `confirm` interact with the existing `code` state.\n\nDesign approach:\n- `begin(now)`: issues a new code AND a confirmToken. Store both in the `StoredCode` (add `confirmHash`, `confirmUsedAt`, maybe `confirmExpiresAt`). Returns `{ code, expiresAt, confirmToken }`.\n  - The `code` is for the QR (phone scans).\n  - The `confirmToken` is a separate random secret (e.g., randomBytes(24) base64url), shown to the computer.\n- `confirm(code, confirmToken, deviceName, now)`: \n  - Look up current code.\n  - Validate code matches (hashEquals).\n  - Validate confirmToken matches (hashEquals).\n  - Validate not expired (both code and confirmToken 5-min).\n  - Validate confirmToken not already used (one-time).\n  - Validate attempts limit.\n  - Issue device token, mark code used + confirmToken used.\n  - Wrong confirmToken or wrong code → increments attempts.\n  - Return RedeemResult | RedeemError.\n\nHmm, but there's the question of \"只有 code 不能完成\". If confirm requires both, then a request with only `code` (no confirmToken) is rejected. Good. But what should the error be? Let me add a new error type `confirm_required` or `bad_confirm`. Actually let me think about the error taxonomy the task wants:\n- two-stage success\n- only code can't complete\n- wrong confirmToken → rejected\n- expired → rejected\n- one-time (replay fails)\n- toggle rollback to single-stage\n\nLet me define error codes:\n- `\"unknown_code\"` - code missing/wrong\n- `\"code_used\"` - code already redeemed (replay)\n- `\"code_expired\"` - expired\n- `\"too_many_attempts\"` - rate limited\n- `\"confirm_required\"` - confirmToken missing (only code provided)\n- `\"bad_confirm\"` / `\"confirm_invalid\"` - confirmToken wrong\n- `\"confirm_used\"` - confirmToken already used\n\nHmm, but let me keep it simpler and aligned with existing `RedeemError`. Let me think about what makes sense.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole design. Maybe cleaner: The `begin` endpoint is the \"first段\". The `confirm` is the \"second段\". \n\nWait — actually, I wonder if the intended design is different. Let me re-read very carefully:\n\n\"第一段：`POST /api/v1/pairing/begin` → 返回 `{ code, expiresAt, confirmToken }`（`code` 是给手机扫的短码，`confirmToken` 是给**电脑端**确认用的，**不随二维码下发**）\"\n\nSo `begin` returns both code and confirmToken. The code goes into QR. The confirmToken is for the computer.\n\n\"第二段：`POST /api/v1/pairing/confirm` body `{ code, confirmToken }` → 才真正发设备令牌.\"\n\nSo `confirm` takes both. \n\n\"1.3 关键安全语义：只扫码（拿到 code）不能完成配对——必须有人在电脑端拿到 confirmToken 并输进去。这就是\"手机绝不代确认\"。\"\n\nOK here's my interpretation of the real flow: \n- The phone scans the QR and gets `code`. \n- The phone connects to the harness and wants to redeem. But with two-step, the phone CANNOT redeem with just `code`. \n- The `confirmToken` is displayed on the computer. Someone reads it and types it into the PHONE (or the phone app displays a field). Wait — \"必须有人在电脑端拿到 confirmToken 并输进去\" = \"someone must get the confirmToken on the computer and type it in\". Hmm, \"在电脑端\" (on the computer side). So the person types the confirmToken on the computer? Or types it into the phone?\n\nActually \"有人在电脑端拿到 confirmToken 并输进去\" — the person gets confirmToken from the computer screen and inputs it. Into where? Possibly into the phone app (the phone shows a \"confirm\" field that the person fills by reading the computer screen). That's the classic \"two-device confirmation\": scan QR on phone, read a code off the trusted device and type it into the phone. This ensures a human at the computer is present.\n\nEither way — from the server's perspective, the `confirm` endpoint requires `{code, confirmToken}` and issues the device token. Who calls it (phone or computer) doesn't matter for the server interface. The security property is: both secrets required, and confirmToken never goes in the QR.\n\nOK so my design is correct. Let me now think about where these endpoints live regarding auth:\n- `begin`: computer calls it to generate QR. Computer is authenticated. So `begin` requires auth (default, not in PAIR_OPEN_PATHS). Good — also this prevents an unauthenticated attacker from generating codes.\n- `confirm`: this issues the device token. The caller has no device credential yet (that's what they're getting). So `confirm` must be open (免鉴权), like `redeem`. Add to PAIR_OPEN_PATHS.\n\nBut wait — the security concern: if `confirm` is open and the confirmToken is guessable, an attacker could brute-force. But the confirmToken is a high-entropy random secret (randomBytes), attempts are rate-limited, and it's one-time/5-min. Plus the code must match. OK.\n\nHmm, but actually there's a subtlety: \"confirmToken 与 code 绑定\" (confirmToken bound to code). So the confirmToken only works with the specific code it was issued with.\n\nLet me also think: \"尝试次数计入既有 attempts 限流\" — attempts count into the EXISTING `attempts` limiter. Currently `redeem` increments `current.attempts` on wrong code. So `confirm` should share the same `attempts` counter on the stored code. Good — that's the \"既有 attempts 限流\" (the existing attempts field, MAX_CODE_ATTEMPTS=5).\n\nNow the switch `PAIR_TWO_STEP` (default true; false → old single-stage). What does the switch control? Per 1.5: \"保留旧的单段 `POST /pairing/redeem`（回滚用），开关 `PAIR_TWO_STEP`（默认 true；false → 走旧单段）\". \n\nSo when `PAIR_TWO_STEP=false`, behavior reverts to old single-stage. How does this manifest? A few interpretations:\n1. `begin`/`confirm` endpoints are disabled (404) when false, and only `redeem` works.\n2. `redeem` works in both modes, but when `PAIR_TWO_STEP=true`, `redeem` is... hmm.\n\nWait. The core security semantic is \"只扫码不能完成配对\" (scanning alone can't complete). If we keep `redeem` (single-stage) always working, then with `PAIR_TWO_STEP=true`, an attacker could just call `redeem` with the code and bypass the two-step! That would defeat the whole purpose.\n\nSo when `PAIR_TWO_STEP=true` (default), the old single-stage `redeem` MUST be disabled or must not issue tokens via code alone. Otherwise \"只扫码不能完成配对\" is a lie.\n\nLet me think. The task says \"保留旧的单段 `POST /pairing/redeem`（回滚用）\". So `redeem` is kept for rollback. When `PAIR_TWO_STEP=true`, `redeem` should be... disabled? Or should it behave differently?\n\nHmm. Let me re-read: \"1.5 保留旧的单段 `POST /pairing/redeem`（回滚用），开关 `PAIR_TWO_STEP`（默认 true；false → 走旧单段）\". \n\nI think the cleanest interpretation:\n- `PAIR_TWO_STEP=true` (default): the two-step endpoints (`begin`/`confirm`) are active. The old `redeem` is NOT usable for completing pairing via code alone — it's disabled (returns an error like `{\"error\":\"two_step_required\"}` or 404). Actually, to honor \"保留...（回滚用）\" — keeping it available but gated.\n- `PAIR_TWO_STEP=false`: `begin`/`confirm` disabled (404 or error), and `redeem` works as before (single-stage).\n\nHmm, but \"保留旧的单段\" means keep the code path. Let me make `redeem` always registered, but when `PAIR_TWO_STEP=true`, calling `redeem` returns an error directing to two-step. Actually — that would break existing clients. But the whole point of two-step is to tighten security, and the task explicitly says \"两段式配对是收紧行为，但为了安全默认开（可用开关回滚）\" and \"手机绝不代确认\". \n\nActually wait — but existing single-stage `redeem` is what the CURRENT phone app uses. If default true disables it, the current phone app breaks. But the task says \"手机端接不接是它的事\" and \"手机端 UI 另说，但接口必须先有\". So the server provides the new interface; the phone will be updated separately. The default (true) enforces the new stricter behavior. That's explicitly acknowledged.\n\nHmm, but let me reconsider — maybe the intent is softer: with `PAIR_TWO_STEP=true`, BOTH flows exist but `redeem` is deprecated. But then \"只扫码不能完成配对\" is violated because `redeem(code)` works. Unless... the two-step `begin` issues a code that's marked as \"two-step\" and `redeem` rejects two-step codes. Hmm, that's getting complex.\n\nLet me think about the cleanest design that satisfies the security test \"只有 code 不能完成\" (only code can't complete) and \"开关回滚到单段\" (toggle back to single-stage):\n\nI'll make the switch control the `redeem` endpoint's behavior at the store level too, OR gate the endpoints. Let me think about what's testable in unit tests (§1.6 tests are on the store, since the http probe tests endpoints).\n\nActually §1.6 says \"单测\" (unit tests). These are on `PairingStore` methods (like pairing.test.ts). So the store needs two-step methods: `begin()` and `confirm()`, plus a switch function `pairTwoStepEnabled()`. The unit tests test:\n- two-step success: begin → confirm(code, confirmToken) → success\n- only code can't complete: confirm with code but no/wrong confirmToken → rejected\n- confirmToken wrong → rejected\n- expired → rejected\n- one-time (replay fails): confirm twice → second fails\n- toggle rollback to single-stage: with PAIR_TWO_STEP=false, redeem(begin's code) works (single-stage)\n\nHmm interesting — \"开关回滚到单段\" test. So when `PAIR_TWO_STEP=false`, the OLD single-stage `redeem` works. \n\nLet me now design the store carefully. I'll introduce:\n\n```ts\nexport function pairTwoStepEnabled(env = process.env): boolean {\n  const raw = (env.PAIR_TWO_STEP ?? \"\").trim().toLowerCase();\n  if (!raw) return true; // default on\n  return raw !== \"false\" && raw !== \"0\" && raw !== \"off\";\n}\n```\n\nStore changes: extend `StoredCode` with optional confirm fields:\n```ts\ninterface StoredCode {\n  hash: string;\n  expiresAt: number;\n  usedAt?: number;\n  attempts: number;\n  // 两段式（2026-11-10 批 28 §一）：电脑端确认令牌，只存 sha256，不随二维码下发\n  confirmHash?: string;\n  confirmUsedAt?: number;\n}\n```\n\n`begin(now)`: \n```ts\nbegin(now = Date.now()): { code: string; expiresAt: number; confirmToken: string } {\n  const code = String(randomInt(0, 1_000_000)).padStart(6, \"0\");\n  const confirmToken = randomBytes(24).toString(\"base64url\");\n  const expiresAt = now + CODE_TTL_SECONDS * 1000; // 5 min (300s default)\n  this.store.code = { \n    hash: sha256(code), \n    expiresAt, \n    attempts: 0,\n    confirmHash: sha256(confirmToken),\n  };\n  this.persist();\n  return { code, expiresAt, confirmToken };\n}\n```\n\nWait — task says confirmToken \"5 分钟过期\". Default CODE_TTL_SECONDS is 300 = 5 min. So I can reuse `expiresAt` for both. But to be explicit, maybe a separate confirm TTL constant of 300. Actually the task says \"confirmToken 一次性、5 分钟过期、与 code 绑定\". The code also has `PAIR_CODE_TTL_SECONDS` default 300. Let me just use the same `expiresAt` (both expire together), which naturally binds them. But to be safe and explicit, I could add a dedicated `confirmExpiresAt`. Hmm — simpler to bind them to the same expiry. But \"与 code 绑定\" (bound to code) is naturally satisfied because the confirmHash is stored INSIDE the same `StoredCode` record. \n\nLet me add a dedicated constant for clarity though. Actually, let me keep it simple: confirmToken lives on the same code record, shares `expiresAt`. That's both \"5分钟\" (since default is 300s) and \"与code绑定\" (same record). Good. But I should double check: is `PAIR_CODE_TTL_SECONDS` configurable to something other than 300? Yes. But the task says 5 min for confirmToken. Hmm. To strictly honor \"5 分钟\", I'll add `CONFIRM_TTL_SECONDS = 300` fixed, and store `confirmExpiresAt = now + 300*1000`, checked independently. Actually that's more precise and auditable. Let me do that. But then code expiry and confirm expiry could differ. Let me check both in confirm:\n- `if (current.expiresAt <= now) return \"code_expired\"` (code expired)\n- `if (current.confirmExpiresAt && current.confirmExpiresAt <= now) return \"confirm_expired\"` \n\nHmm, that adds an error type. Let me simplify: task test says \"过期 → 拒绝\" (expired → rejected). I can return \"code_expired\" for both. Let me just use one expiry (shared) to keep it clean, OR add confirmExpiresAt but map to \"code_expired\"/\"confirm_expired\". \n\nActually let me reconsider. I think the cleanest: store `confirmExpiresAt` (fixed 5 min) and check it, but return `code_expired` on either expiry to avoid proliferating error codes... no, that's confusing. \n\nLet me step back and keep it genuinely simple. I'll bind confirmToken to the code record and use ONE expiry (`expiresAt`). The default `PAIR_CODE_TTL_SECONDS=300` IS 5 minutes. So \"confirmToken 5 分钟过期\" is satisfied by default. If someone changes `PAIR_CODE_TTL_SECONDS`, both change together — that's acceptable and arguably more correct (they're a pair). I'll document that they share the TTL. This avoids a separate constant and a separate error code. The test \"过期 → 拒绝\" can advance `now` past `expiresAt` and assert rejection.\n\nHmm, but wait — to be rigorous about \"5 分钟\" being independent and explicit, let me add a dedicated constant but still store on the same record. Actually, you know what, let me add `CONFIRM_TTL_SECONDS = 300` and `confirmExpiresAt` to be explicit and independently testable, and add a distinct error `\"confirm_expired\"` mapped to 410. Actually, having distinct error codes is cleaner for debugging. Let me think about the HTTP status mapping.\n\nLet me enumerate errors for `confirm`:\n- `\"unknown_code\"` (404) — code missing/wrong (or code record missing)\n- `\"confirm_required\"` (400) — confirmToken missing entirely (only code provided) — this is the \"只有 code 不能完成\" case\n- `\"bad_confirm\"` / `\"confirm_mismatch\"` (403? 400?) — confirmToken wrong. Hmm, 403 is for auth failures with empty body per hard constraint \"鉴权失败一律 403 空体\". But this is a business rejection (bad input), should be 400. Let me use 400 for business rejections. Actually wait — let me re-check the hard constraint: \"鉴权失败一律 403 空体，不区分原因；业务拒绝才 400 带说明。\" A wrong confirmToken is a business rejection (it's part of the pairing request body, not the Bearer auth). So 400. But actually — hmm, is a wrong confirmToken an \"auth failure\"? It's not the `AGENT_TOKEN` auth. It's a pairing code validation, same category as `unknown_code` (404) and `code_expired` (410). So 400/404/410 are fine.\n- `\"code_used\"` / `\"confirm_used\"` (409) — replay (one-time)\n- `\"code_expired\"` (410) — expired\n- `\"too_many_attempts\"` (429) — rate limited\n\nLet me keep the error set minimal and reuse existing ones where possible. Actually, let me define a new error union for confirm, reusing `RedeemError` strings plus new ones. Hmm. Let me think about what's cleanest.\n\nActually, I'll make `confirm` return the same `RedeemResult | RedeemError` but extend `RedeemError` with new members:\n```ts\nexport type ConfirmError =\n  | \"unknown_code\"\n  | \"confirm_required\"      // 只给了 code，没给 confirmToken\n  | \"bad_confirm\"           // confirmToken 错\n  | \"code_used\"             // 重放（code 或 confirm 已用过）\n  | \"code_expired\"\n  | \"confirm_expired\"\n  | \"too_many_attempts\";\n```\n\nHmm, that's a lot. Let me simplify by having `RedeemError` extended. Actually, let me define a separate `ConfirmResult` type to keep `redeem`'s contract unchanged (important — don't break existing single-stage). Actually `RedeemError` is a type used in `redeem`'s return. If I extend it, the existing `redeem` switch in index.ts handles all cases. Adding new members would require the switch's `else` to handle them. The existing switch uses a ternary mapping to status; new members would fall into the `429` default. That's a bug risk. So I'll keep `RedeemError` unchanged and define a new `ConfirmError` type for the two-step confirm method. \n\nWait, but actually `redeem` and `confirm` share a lot. Let me think about whether `confirm` should internally call a common device-issuing helper. Yes — to avoid duplication, I'll factor the device-issuing logic into a private method `issueDevice(deviceName, now)` and have both `redeem` and `confirm` call it after their respective validations. That reduces duplication and keeps behavior identical. Good — but careful, AGENTS.md says \"不做与本需求无关的重构\". Factoring out `issueDevice` is a minimal refactor directly needed to avoid duplicating device issuance in the new `confirm` method. It's justified. But I need to be careful not to change `redeem`'s behavior. Let me keep `redeem` mostly as-is and just have `confirm` reuse a helper. Actually, to minimize risk to existing `redeem`, let me extract the device-issuing block into a private method and have both call it — verifying the resulting `redeem` behavior is byte-identical.\n\nHmm, actually let me reconsider — maybe simpler to NOT refactor `redeem` and just write `confirm` with its own device-issuing code (duplicating ~15 lines). The AGENTS.md warns against over-engineering and unrelated refactors, but also duplication. Extracting a private helper used by both is clean and low-risk. I'll do the extraction but carefully preserve `redeem`.\n\nActually, you know, let me reconsider the whole thing. Let me reconsider whether confirm should even issue a device via a shared path. The `redeem` method currently:\n1. validates code\n2. generates token, hash, id, device\n3. marks code usedAt\n4. trims devices to MAX_DEVICES\n5. persists\n6. returns result\n\nI'll extract steps 2-6 into `private issueDevice(deviceName, now): RedeemResult` that also marks `current.usedAt = now`. Then:\n- `redeem`: validate → `issueDevice`\n- `confirm`: validate code + confirmToken → `issueDevice`\n\nLet me write it.\n\nNow, about `begin` vs `issueCode`: `issueCode` is used by `/pair`, `/api/v1/pair/qr`, `/api/v1/pair/code`, and the pairing.test.ts `pairOne` helper. I should NOT change `issueCode`'s signature/behavior (existing tests depend on it returning `{code, expiresAt}`). So `begin` is a NEW method. Good. That keeps existing endpoints working (single-stage path) regardless.\n\nHold on — but if `issueCode` (used by `/pair`, `/pair/qr`, `/pair/code`) does NOT generate a confirmToken, then in two-step mode, a code generated via `/pair/qr` has no confirmToken and can't be confirmed. That means the two-step flow REQUIRES using `begin`. That's fine — `begin` is the new two-step entry point. The QR from `/pair/qr` (old) would generate a single-stage-only code. \n\nBut wait — security concern: with `PAIR_TWO_STEP=true`, if `/pair/qr` generates a code WITHOUT confirmToken, and `redeem` is still open... an attacker who gets that code from the QR could call `redeem(code)` and complete pairing with just the QR! That violates \"只扫码不能完成配对\". \n\nSo I MUST gate `redeem` when `PAIR_TWO_STEP=true`. Let me make `redeem` (the store method and/or the endpoint) reject when two-step is enabled. \n\nOption A: Gate at the HTTP endpoint level: when `PAIR_TWO_STEP=true`, `/api/v1/pair/redeem` returns `{\"error\":\"two_step_required\"}` (or 404/410) telling clients to use begin/confirm. When false, it works as before.\n\nOption B: Gate at the store `redeem()` method: check `pairTwoStepEnabled()` and return an error. But the store method is also used by `pairOne` in pairing.test.ts (single-stage tests). Those tests don't set PAIR_TWO_STEP, so default true would break them! \n\nHmm. That's a problem. The existing pairing.test.ts `pairOne` uses `issueCode()` + `redeem()`. If I gate `redeem()` by `PAIR_TWO_STEP` default-true, all existing pairing tests break.\n\nSo I should NOT gate the store `redeem()` method by the switch. Instead, gate at the HTTP endpoint. The store `redeem()` remains a pure single-stage primitive (used by tests and by the old endpoint). The switch governs which HTTP endpoints are active.\n\nBut also §1.6 says unit test \"开关回滚到单段\" — \"toggle rollback to single-stage\". If the switch only lives in index.ts (HTTP), then a unit test on the store can't test it. Hmm. Unless I put a testable switch function `pairTwoStepEnabled()` in pairing.ts (exported) and unit-test THAT function, plus the store's begin/confirm. And the \"rollback to single-stage\" behavior is tested at... hmm.\n\nLet me reconsider. Maybe the cleanest design: put the two-step logic ENTIRELY in pairing.ts (store), with the switch `pairTwoStepEnabled()` there, and have the HTTP endpoints just wire it up. The unit tests test the store methods AND the switch function. The \"开关回滚到单段\" test could test that `redeem()` still works (single-stage) when the switch is off, or test the switch function directly.\n\nHmm, but the security semantic \"只扫码不能完成\" needs to hold at the HTTP level too. Let me think about the full picture with the switch lives in index.ts vs pairing.ts.\n\nActually, here's a cleaner idea: make the switch control WHICH method the `confirm`/`redeem` endpoints use, but ALSO — critically — the security semantic is enforced by the design of `begin`/`confirm`: a code issued by `begin` carries a `confirmHash`, and `redeem()` REFUSES to redeem a code that has a `confirmHash` (i.e., a two-step code must go through `confirm`). \n\nWait, that's actually elegant! Let me think:\n- `begin()` issues a code WITH a `confirmHash` (two-step code).\n- `issueCode()` (old) issues a code WITHOUT `confirmHash` (single-stage code).\n- `redeem()` (single-stage) checks: if the current code has a `confirmHash`, it means this code was issued for two-step and MUST be confirmed — so `redeem` returns an error like `\"confirm_required\"`. This enforces \"只扫码不能完成\" — because any code from the two-step QR (via begin) cannot be redeemed via the single-stage path.\n\nBut wait — the QR for two-step comes from `begin`'s `code`. Where's the QR generated? The task says `begin` returns `code` + `confirmToken`, and \"confirmToken 不随二维码下发\". So the QR is built from `code`. But currently QR is built by `/pair` and `/api/v1/pair/qr` using `issueCode()`. For two-step, we'd need a QR from `begin`'s code. \n\nHmm. The task §1.2 says \"服务端实现两段式（手机端 UI 另说，但接口必须先有）\". And \"接口必须先有\" — the interfaces must exist. It doesn't explicitly require wiring up a two-step QR page. But it would be good to provide it. Actually, task §2 is the UI work for old-session recall. §1 is pairing server interfaces. So for §1, I need `begin` and `confirm` endpoints. The QR generation — I could make `begin` also return a URL/dataUrl, OR add a `begin` variant that includes the QR. But the task's `begin` response shape is strictly `{ code, expiresAt, confirmToken }`. So I shouldn't add extra fields to `begin` (well, adding fields is \"纯增量\" but let me stick to the spec). \n\nActually, re-reading: \"返回 `{ code, expiresAt, confirmToken }`\". I'll return exactly those three (maybe I can add `url` as pure increment? The spec is explicit with three fields. Let me return exactly those to match §1.6's \"搜索端点形状\" style tests... no, that's §2.4). Let me return exactly `{ code, expiresAt, confirmToken }` for `begin`. The computer can build the QR from `code` using `buildPairUrl`. Actually — the desktop设置页 needs a QR. Hmm. Let me think: does the task require the QR to be generated server-side for two-step? \n\n\"只扫码不能完成配对\" — the QR contains `code` only. So someone needs to build a QR from `code`. The existing `/api/v1/pair/qr` builds a QR from a NEW code via `issueCode()`. For two-step, I think the cleanest is to ALSO provide a QR endpoint or have `begin` return enough. But adding extra is scope creep.\n\nLet me reconsider: maybe I should make the `/pair/qr` and `/pair` endpoints (which generate the QR) switch to using `begin()` when `PAIR_TWO_STEP=true`, so the QR code they show is a two-step code, and the `confirmToken` is... hmm, but then where does the confirmToken go? The QR endpoint returns JSON to the browser (authenticated). The browser could display the confirmToken. \n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Let me step back and focus on the EXPLICIT requirements, which are about server interfaces and unit tests, not about a complete polished pairing UX (that's \"手机端 UI 另说\").\n\nExplicit §1 requirements:\n1. `POST /api/v1/pairing/begin` → `{ code, expiresAt, confirmToken }`\n2. `POST /api/v1/pairing/confirm` body `{ code, confirmToken }` → issues device token\n3. Only scanning (code) can't complete — must have confirmToken\n4. confirmToken: one-time, 5-min, bound to code; attempts into existing rate limiting\n5. Keep old single-stage `/pair/redeem` for rollback; switch `PAIR_TWO_STEP` default true\n6. Unit tests: 6 cases\n\nNote requirement 1.5 says \"保留旧的单段 `POST /pairing/redeem`（回滚用）\". The existing endpoint is `/api/v1/pair/redeem`. I'll keep it as-is (it already exists). But I need to make sure that when `PAIR_TWO_STEP=true`, the single-stage redeem can't be used to bypass two-step with a two-step code. \n\nDesign decision: I'll make `begin()` generate a code with a `confirmHash`, and make `redeem()` reject codes that have a `confirmHash` (returning `\"confirm_required\"`). This way:\n- A two-step code (from begin) can ONLY be redeemed via `confirm`.\n- The old `redeem` still works for old-style codes (from `issueCode`) — but in two-step mode, all QR-generating endpoints should use `begin` so their codes are two-step codes.\n\nHmm wait, but actually if `/pair` and `/pair/qr` still use `issueCode()` (no confirmHash), then in two-step mode those QRs produce single-stage codes redeemable via `redeem` → bypass. So I need to gate `redeem` at the HTTP level by the switch too. \n\nLet me simplify with a clear, defensible rule:\n\n**The switch `PAIR_TWO_STEP` gates the HTTP endpoints:**\n- When `true` (default): \n  - `begin` / `confirm` are active.\n  - `/api/v1/pair/redeem` (single-stage) is DISABLED — returns `410 {\"error\":\"two_step_required\"}` (or similar), because allowing it would let a QR-only scan complete pairing.\n  - All existing QR/code endpoints (`/pair`, `/pair/qr`, `/pair/code`) switch to `begin()` so their codes are two-step codes. Wait — but then those endpoints' responses change shape (they'd need to expose confirmToken, which shouldn't go in the QR but CAN be shown on the authenticated computer page). Hmm. Actually the `/pair` page and `/api/v1/pair/qr` are called by the COMPUTER (authenticated browser / desktop). So exposing confirmToken there is fine (it's the computer side). The QR dataUrl still only encodes `code`. So I can have `/pair/qr` call `begin()` and add `confirmToken` to the JSON response (the browser shows it as text, not in QR). And `/pair` page shows the confirmToken as text for the human to read. \n\nHmm, but that's expanding scope into the pair-page.ts and QR endpoints. Let me check — is that necessary? \n\nActually, let me reconsider. The task says \"手机端 UI 另说，但接口必须先有\" and \"服务端接口先就位\". The emphasis is on the INTERFACES (begin/confirm). It does NOT require me to rewire the QR page. The QR page generating single-stage codes is a SEPARATE concern. But if I leave `/pair/qr` using `issueCode()` (single-stage) with `redeem` disabled in two-step mode, then the existing QR pairing flow BREAKS (the code can't be redeemed). That's a regression for the current phone app.\n\nHmm. This is the tension: two-step DEFAULT ON breaks the current single-stage phone flow. The task explicitly acknowledges this: \"两段式配对是收紧行为，但为了安全默认开（可用开关回滚）\" and \"手机绝不代确认\" and \"手机端接不接是它的事\". So breaking the old phone flow by default is INTENDED. The rollback switch `PAIR_TWO_STEP=false` restores it.\n\nOK so given that, here's my plan — keep it MINIMAL and focused on the interfaces:\n\n**pairing.ts** (store + switch):\n- Add `pairTwoStepEnabled(env)` exported function (default true).\n- Extend `StoredCode` with optional `confirmHash?` and `confirmUsedAt?`.\n- Add `begin(now)` → `{ code, expiresAt, confirmToken }`: issues code + confirmToken (both hashed, stored on the code record). \n- Add `confirm(code, confirmToken, deviceName, now)` → `ConfirmResult | ConfirmError`: validates both secrets, issues device.\n- Refactor device issuance into a private helper shared by `redeem` and `confirm`.\n- **Security**: `redeem()` refuses codes that carry a `confirmHash` (two-step codes) — returns `\"confirm_required\"`. Wait, but `redeem` is used by `pairOne` test with `issueCode()` codes (no confirmHash) → still works. Good. And it prevents a two-step code from being redeemed single-stage. \n\nHmm, but actually, do I even need the `redeem` guard if I gate the endpoint? Let me think. If `PAIR_TWO_STEP=true` disables the `/pair/redeem` endpoint entirely, then no one can call `redeem` via HTTP in two-step mode, so a two-step code can't be redeemed via HTTP. The guard in the store is defense-in-depth. But it's a nice security property: even if someone calls `redeem()` directly (or via a future endpoint), a two-step code can't be single-stage redeemed. Let me include it — it strengthens \"只扫码不能完成\" at the data layer. Actually wait — will it break the §1.6 \"开关回滚到单段\" test? Let me design that test carefully.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider the \"开关回滚到单段\" test. What does \"回滚到单段\" mean at the store level? With `PAIR_TWO_STEP=false`, the single-stage `redeem` should work. But my `redeem` guard is about `confirmHash`, not the switch. So with switch false, `issueCode()` codes still work via `redeem`. And `begin()` codes (with confirmHash) would still be rejected by `redeem`. But in rollback mode, `begin`/`confirm` endpoints wouldn't be used. So the test \"开关回滚到单段\" would verify: with `PAIR_TWO_STEP=false`, the old flow `issueCode() → redeem()` works (which it always does), and maybe that `begin`/`confirm` are the gated ones.\n\nActually, I think the switch should primarily be tested as a pure function: `pairTwoStepEnabled({}) === true`, `pairTwoStepEnabled({PAIR_TWO_STEP:\"false\"}) === false`. And the HTTP-level gating is tested in the http probe. Let me check whether I should add http probe tests. The task says \"pnpm --filter @lifediary/server http 全绿\" — must pass. It doesn't explicitly say to ADD http probe tests. But §1.6 and §2.4 say \"单测\" (unit tests). So unit tests go in .test.ts files. The http probe is a separate integration test. I should make sure it stays green (doesn't break) and ideally add coverage. But modifying http-probe.ts is a big file; adding tests there is optional. Let me focus on unit tests first, then decide about http probe.\n\nActually — wait. Let me reconsider the endpoint gating. If I DISABLE `/api/v1/pair/redeem` when `PAIR_TWO_STEP=true`, will that break the http probe §30 (pairing fingerprint test) which currently does `pair/code` → `pair/redeem`? Let me check the http probe. The batch 26a change record says \"§30 配对指纹（redeem 下发 → 清单同值...）\". So the http probe DOES use `/api/v1/pair/redeem`. If I disable it in two-step mode (default), that probe test would break unless the probe sets `PAIR_TWO_STEP=false` or I keep redeem working.\n\nHmm. This is important. Let me look at the http probe's pairing section and how it sets env / starts the server. Let me read the http-probe.ts.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole gating decision. There's a real tension:\n- Task wants two-step default-ON with \"只扫码不能完成\" security.\n- Existing http probe §30 uses single-stage redeem.\n- Existing phone app uses single-stage redeem.\n\nIf I disable redeem by default, I break the http probe (need to set env in probe) and the phone app. But the task explicitly says the phone app will be updated separately and the default is intentionally stricter. For the http probe, I can set `PAIR_TWO_STEP=false` in the probe's env for the §30 test, OR keep redeem functional.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider: maybe the intended design is LESS disruptive. Let me reconsider \"1.5 保留旧的单段 `POST /pairing/redeem`（回滚用），开关 `PAIR_TWO_STEP`（默认 true；false → 走旧单段）\".\n\nMaybe the interpretation is: `redeem` is ALWAYS available (kept). The switch `PAIR_TWO_STEP` controls whether `begin`/`confirm` are available AND whether `redeem` requires the two-step. Hmm.\n\nActually, maybe simpler: the switch controls the behavior of `redeem`:\n- `PAIR_TWO_STEP=true`: `redeem(code)` alone does NOT issue a token — it requires a confirmToken. OR `redeem` is disabled.\n- `PAIR_TWO_STEP=false`: `redeem(code)` works single-stage.\n\nAnd `begin`/`confirm` are the new endpoints for the true mode.\n\nHmm, let me think about \"只扫码不能完成\". If `redeem` is kept open and in true mode it returns \"confirm_required\" when called with only code, that satisfies \"只扫码不能完成\" for the `redeem` path. And `confirm` requires both. \n\nLet me reconsider — what if I DON'T disable redeem, but instead make `redeem` in two-step mode reject (return an error telling to confirm)? That keeps the endpoint \"present\" (rollback just flips behavior) and satisfies security. And I can keep the http probe §30 working by having it set `PAIR_TWO_STEP=false`... but that requires editing the probe.\n\nUgh, let me just look at the http probe to understand the pairing test and how env is controlled. That will inform the design. Let me read it."
      }
