        "text": "`getHarness` is re-exported from `agent.ts`. I can import it in index.ts. Good.\n\nNow let me think about the search endpoint design. The new endpoint `GET /api/v1/agent/sessions/search?q=&days=&limit=` should:\n- Reuse `searchSessions(getHarness(), q, {days, limit})`.\n- Return hits (shape from `SessionSearchHit`: `{conversationId, title, at, snippet}`).\n- Validate: empty/short query → 400 (task §2.4: \"空查询 400\"). Wait — the existing `/api/v1/agent/search` returns `{hits: []}` for short queries. But the NEW endpoint spec says \"空查询 400\". Let me re-read §2.4: \"单测：搜索端点形状 / 空查询 400 / 结果不含正文超长截断\". So the new endpoint should return 400 for empty query. Hmm, but `searchTranscriptLines` returns `[]` for queries < 2 chars. And `searchSessions` returns `[]` for < 2 chars. The task wants \"空查询 400\" — so the endpoint should reject empty (or < 2 char) queries with 400. Let me think: \"空查询\" = empty query. I'll return 400 for empty/whitespace query (q.trim().length === 0), and maybe also for < 2 chars? The §2.4 test says \"空查询 400\" — I'll make empty → 400, and short (<2) → 400 too (consistent, since <2 returns no hits anyway). Actually, let me be careful: `recall_sessions` uses \"短于 2 字直接空\" (short queries return empty). For the HTTP endpoint, the task explicitly wants \"空查询 400\". Let me interpret as: q missing/empty/whitespace → 400. For 1-char queries, I could return `{hits: []}` (consistent with searchTranscriptLines) or 400. To be safe and match \"空查询 400\", I'll return 400 when `q.trim().length < 2` — no wait, that changes the semantics for 1-char. Hmm. Let me think about what a unit test would check: \"空查询 400\" → `GET ...?q=` → 400. I'll implement: if `q.trim().length < 2` → 400 `{error:\"bad_request\", message:\"q 至少 2 个字符\"}`. That covers empty AND short. Actually that's cleaner — one rule: q must be ≥2 chars. But wait, will that break anything? It's a new endpoint, no existing clients. Fine. But hold on — `searchSessions` itself returns `[]` for <2 chars, so the endpoint would just never call it for <2. OK.\n\nHmm, but actually — should I make the endpoint return 400 for <2 or just for empty? Let me re-read: \"空查询 400\". I'll implement q missing or `q.trim().length === 0` → 400. And for 1-char (non-empty but short), `searchSessions` returns `[]`, so `{hits: []}`. Actually, to keep it simple and avoid a weird gap, let me do: `if (q.trim().length < 2) return 400`. I'll document it. Hmm, but that means a 1-char search returns 400 instead of empty hits. That's a reasonable \"bad_request\". Actually, I worry a test might check `?q=a` → `{hits:[]}`. But the task doesn't mention that. Let me go with: empty/whitespace → 400; 1-char → pass to searchSessions (returns []) → `{hits:[]}`. Wait, that's inconsistent (empty→400 but 1-char→200 empty). Hmm.\n\nLet me reconsider. The cleanest define: q must have ≥ 2 non-whitespace chars (same threshold as the search core), else 400. This is a clean, single rule and matches \"空查询 400\" (empty is < 2). I'll go with that. Actually — hmm, one concern: the `searchTranscriptLines` returns [] for <2, meaning the CORE treats <2 as \"no hits\" not \"error\". The endpoint adding a 400 for <2 is a boundary validation (good — \"validate at system boundaries\"). I'll do it.\n\nWait, actually, let me reconsider to minimize surprises: task says \"空查询 400\" specifically. The safest minimal implementation that definitely satisfies it: `if (!q || q.trim().length === 0) return c.json({error:\"bad_request\", message:\"q 不能为空\"}, 400)`. And for 1-char queries, let searchSessions handle (returns []). But then a 1-char query returns 200 `{hits:[]}`. That's fine and consistent with \"short queries yield nothing\". Hmm. But is it weird that empty → 400 but 1-char → 200? A reviewer might find it odd but it's defensible: empty is a malformed request (missing required param), while 1-char is a valid-but-too-short query that yields no results (matching the tool's semantics). \n\nActually, I'll go with the threshold-2 rule: `q.trim().length < 2 → 400`. Rationale: the search core explicitly says \"短于 2 字直接空\", so the endpoint should reject <2 as bad_request rather than silently returning empty (which would mislead the UI into thinking \"no matches\" when actually the query was too short). This aligns with \"不冒充已知\" and boundary validation. I'll document it. Hmm, but actually — wait, would that conflict with \"结果不含正文超长截断\"? No, that's about snippet truncation.\n\nLet me also reconsider: \"结果不含正文超长截断\" (results don't contain over-long body / truncation). The `SessionSearchHit.snippet` is already capped at 200 chars (`SNIPPET_MAX_CHARS`) with redaction. So \"结果不含正文超长\" is satisfied by reusing `searchSessions`. I should ensure the endpoint doesn't add the full body. The hits only contain `conversationId, title, at, snippet`. Good. The §2.4 test \"结果不含正文超长截断\" likely checks that `snippet.length <= 200`. I'll write a unit test for that using `searchTranscriptLines` (pure function) — actually that's already tested in session-search.test.ts. But the task wants a NEW unit test for the SEARCH ENDPOINT shape. Hmm — \"搜索端点形状\" (search endpoint shape). Unit-testing an HTTP endpoint requires starting the app... which is hard in a .test.ts (index.ts starts a server on import). \n\nHmm. How do other endpoint tests work? Let me check — e.g., memory-api.test.ts, approval-api.test.ts. They probably test the mounted routes via a Hono app they construct, OR test pure helper functions. Let me look at how an existing api test works, e.g., `flags.test.ts` or `memory-api.test.ts`. Actually, many \"*-api.test.ts\" might construct a Hono app and call `app.request()`. Let me check.\n\nActually — for the search endpoint, I think the cleanest testable design is to extract a PURE helper function (e.g., `parseSessionSearchQuery` and/or a `runSessionSearch` function) into a small module, and unit-test that. But the endpoint uses `getHarness()` (async, durable). Let me think.\n\nLet me reconsider: the task §2.4 \"单测：搜索端点形状 / 空查询 400 / 结果不含正文超长截断\". These are about the endpoint's response shape and validation. To test them without starting the full server, I can:\n- Extract the query validation + clamping + response shaping into a pure function in a new module (e.g., `session-search-api.ts`) that takes a `SessionReader`-like function and returns `{status, body}`. Then unit-test it with a fake reader. And mount the endpoint in index.ts using that helper.\n\nHmm, but that adds a new file. AGENTS.md says \"NEVER create files unless absolutely necessary\" (that's the system prompt) and \"ALWAYS prefer editing an existing file\". But the project structure has many `*-api.ts` files. A new `session-search-api.ts` would be consistent with the repo's patterns. But is it \"absolutely necessary\"? \n\nAlternative: put the pure helper functions directly in `agent/session-search.ts` (which I CAN edit — it's not in the forbidden list) and unit-test them there (add to session-search.test.ts). Then index.ts imports them. That avoids a new file and reuses the existing module. \n\nLet me design the helper in `session-search.ts`:\n- `parseSessionSearchQuery(q: string | undefined, days, limit)` → validates & clamps, returns `{ ok: true, query, options } | { ok: false, error: \"bad_request\", message }`. Actually let me keep it focused.\n- Actually, the response shape is just `{ hits }`. The validation is \"empty → 400\". Let me add a pure function `sessionSearchParams(raw)` that returns either a validated `{query, days, limit}` or an error. And I can test it directly.\n\nHmm, but actually — let me reconsider. The §2.4 tests are \"单测\" (unit tests). The simplest place: add tests to `session-search.test.ts` for:\n1. \"搜索端点形状\" — test that `searchSessions` returns hits with the right shape `{conversationId, title, at, snippet}` and snippet ≤ 200. Actually session-search.test.ts already tests searchTranscriptLines shape. Let me check what it covers.\n\nLet me read session-search.test.ts fully."
