ecall sessions tool\". Hmm.\n\nLet me keep it simple and aligned: I'll add a small dedicated switch `sessionSearchApiEnabled(env)` with env `AGENT_SESSIONS_SEARCH` (default on), default behavior. Actually, you know, to avoid switch proliferation and keep it minimal, let me reuse `recallSessionsEnabled` for gating the endpoint too, and document that `AGENT_RECALL_SESSIONS=false` disables both tool and HTTP endpoint (one line rollback for the whole feature). That's clean and matches \"一行回滚\". Hmm, but the concern: `recallSessionsEnabled` is about the TOOL. Reusing it for the HTTP endpoint is a reasonable extension of \"the recall feature\". Let me do that — actually no. Let me think once more.\n\nHmm, the cleanest for a reviewer: a dedicated, explicit switch for the NEW endpoint, because the endpoint is a new capability. But reuse is fewer moving parts. The AGENTS.md example (`CHANCELLOR_ENABLED=false → 回到 8 个工具`) suggests one toggle can gate a whole feature surface. \n\nI'll go with reusing `recallSessionsEnabled()` to gate the endpoint. Rationale: both are \"会话考古/recall_sessions\" capability; one switch `AGENT_RECALL_SESSIONS=false` rolls back the entire feature (tool + HTTP endpoint). This is the \"一行回滚\" for the whole capability. I'll document it. Actually — wait, but that might be surprising: someone sets AGENT_RECALL_SESSIONS=false to remove the model tool, and accidentally also kills the UI \"找旧事\" feature. But that's arguably intended (disable the whole feature). Fine. Actually, let me reconsider — I'll make it a dedicated switch to be safe and explicit, since §2 is a distinct UI feature. Ugh, I keep flip-flopping.\n\nDecision: dedicated switch `sessionSearchApiEnabled` + env `AGENT_SESSIONS_SEARCH`, default on. It's the cleanest \"new capability gets its own rollback line\" per AGENTS.md, and isolates concerns (turning off the model tool doesn't silently break the UI). I'll add it to session-search.ts next to `recallSessionsEnabled`.\n\nHmm, wait — actually, is a switch even required by the task? §2 doesn't mention one. But AGENTS.md hard constraint says \"新能力给回滚开关\". So yes, I'll add one. Good.\n\nNow, §2.4 unit tests — I'll add to `session-search.test.ts`:\n1. `parseSessionSearchQuery`: shape — valid q returns `{query, options}`; empty/短 → null; days/limit clamping via `searchSessions`.\n2. \"空查询 400\" — test that empty q → `parseSessionSearchQuery` returns null (which the endpoint maps to 400).\n3. \"结果不含正文超长截断\" — integrate: fake reader with a long line, call `searchSessions` with parsed options, assert snippet ≤ 200 and that the full body isn't in the response (only snippet). Actually I'll assert the hit shape has exactly the four keys and snippet ≤ 200.\n\nHmm, but \"搜索端点形状\" — I want to test the endpoint RESPONSE shape `{ hits: [...] }`. If I test `parseSessionSearchQuery` + `searchSessions`, I get the hits but not the `{hits}` wrapper. The wrapper is trivial (`c.json({hits})`). Let me create a small pure function that produces the full response so the test can assert the exact shape:\n\nActually, let me define in session-search.ts:\n```ts\n/**\n * 只读搜索端点的纯装配（§2）：把 query 参数 + reader 解析成最终响应。\n * 返回 {status, body}，index.ts 直接 c.json(body, status)，单测不必起 Hono。\n */\nexport async function sessionSearchResponse(\n  reader: SessionReader,\n  raw: { q?: string; days?: string; limit?: string },\n  now: number = Date.now(),\n): Promise<{ status: number; body: { hits: SessionSearchHit[] } | { error: string; message: string } }> {\n  const parsed = parseSessionSearchQuery(raw);\n  if (!parsed) return { status: 400, body: { error: \"bad_request\", message: \"q 至少 2 个字符\" } };\n  const hits = await searchSessions(reader, parsed.query, parsed.options, now);\n  return { status: 200, body: { hits } };\n}\n```\nThen index.ts:\n```ts\napp.get(\"/api/v1/agent/sessions/search\", async (c) => {\n  if (!sessionSearchApiEnabled()) return c.json({ error: \"not_found\" }, 404);\n  const reader = await getHarness();\n  const result = await sessionSearchResponse(reader, {\n    q: c.req.query(\"q\") ?? undefined,\n    days: c.req.query(\"days\") ?? undefined,\n    limit: c.req.query(\"limit\") ?? undefined,\n  });\n  return c.json(result.body, result.status as 200 | 400);\n});\n```\n\nHmm, wait — but if q < 2, we call `getHarness()` BEFORE validating, which opens durable unnecessarily. Better to validate first. Let me restructure: parse first in index.ts, then getHarness only if valid. But then `sessionSearchResponse` would need to take the reader lazily. Hmm. Alternatively, `sessionSearchResponse` takes a `() => Promise<SessionReader>` factory and only calls it after validation. That's cleaner:\n\n```ts\nexport async function sessionSearchResponse(\n  readerFactory: () => Promise<SessionReader>,\n  raw,\n  now,\n): Promise<...> {\n  const parsed = parseSessionSearchQuery(raw);\n  if (!parsed) return { status: 400, ... };\n  const reader = await readerFactory();\n  const hits = await searchSessions(reader, parsed.query, parsed.options, now);\n  return { status: 200, body: { hits } };\n}\n```\n\nBut that complicates the test (needs a fake factory — trivial though). Hmm. Actually, opening the harness on a 400 request is harmless but wasteful. But actually — `getHarness()` might FAIL if not configured (no LLM), throwing, which would turn a clean 400 into a 500. That's bad. So validate BEFORE getHarness. \n\nLet me restructure so validation happens in index.ts (cheap) and the heavy part is conditional. Actually, simplest: keep `parseSessionSearchQuery` exported and used in index.ts directly for the 400 decision, then build hits. And for the unit test of \"shape\", test `parseSessionSearchQuery` + a helper that shapes hits. Hmm, this is getting convoluted.\n\nLet me simplify: I'll make `sessionSearchResponse` take an optional reader that's only used if validation passes. For the test, pass a fake reader directly. For production, I need to avoid calling getHarness before validation. Option: in index.ts, do validation inline first:\n\n```ts\napp.get(\"/api/v1/agent/sessions/search\", async (c) => {\n  if (!sessionSearchApiEnabled()) return c.json({ error: \"not_found\" }, 404);\n  const params = {\n    q: c.req.query(\"q\") ?? undefined,\n    days: c.req.query(\"days\") ?? undefined,\n    limit: c.req.query(\"limit\") ?? undefined,\n  };\n  const parsed = parseSessionSearchQuery(params);\n  if (!parsed) return c.json({ error: \"bad_request\", message: \"q 至少 2 个字符\" }, 400);\n  const hits = await searchSessions(await getHarness(), parsed.query, parsed.options);\n  return c.json({ hits });\n});\n```\n\nAnd for unit tests, I test `parseSessionSearchQuery` (shape + 400) and `searchSessions`/`searchTranscriptLines` (snippet truncation with a fake reader). That covers all three §2.4 points without needing a fake Hono app. \"搜索端点形状\" = test that `parseSessionSearchQuery` yields the right `{query, options}` shape AND that `searchSessions` returns hits with the right shape. I'll write a test that constructs a fake reader, calls `searchSessions`, and asserts each hit has exactly `{conversationId, title, at, snippet}` keys and snippet ≤ 200. That effectively tests \"端点形状\" (the hits the endpoint returns). Good.\n\nActually — hmm, but the response wrapper `{ hits }` — I could add a tiny pure helper `sessionSearchBody(hits)` → `{ hits }` and test it, but that's over-engineering. Let me just have the test assert the hits shape and note the endpoint wraps as `{hits}`. Actually, to be thorough about \"端点形状\", let me make the endpoint's shape testable by extracting a `formatSessionSearchHits(hits)` that returns `{ hits }` — no, too trivial. \n\nOK let me settle: I'll write §2.4 unit tests in `session-search.test.ts` covering:\n- `parseSessionSearchQuery`: valid → `{query, options}` with clamping; empty/short → null (the \"空查询 400\" precondition).\n- endpoint shape + truncation: build a fake `SessionReader` with a long line, `await searchSessions(...)`, assert `hits` array shape (`{conversationId, title, at, snippet}` exactly, snippet ≤ 200, no full body). \n\nThat's solid. And I'll ALSO add an http probe test for the new endpoint (real HTTP) to verify the actual `{hits}` response and 400 — actually, let me decide on http probe additions later.\n\nNow let me think about §2 frontend. I need to:\n- Add a \"找旧事\" (find old stuff) entry in 设置 → 会话 (or task center).\n- Input keyword → call `GET /api/v1/agent/sessions/search?q=&days=&limit=`.\n- Result list → click one → use `aroundIndex` to pull context → show modal (read-only, no fork).\n- Only edit `web/app.js` + `web/style.css`.\n- `node --check` must pass.\n\nLet me look at the web app.js structure — it's a big file (14000+ lines). Let me understand its structure: how settings sections are built, how modals are created, how API calls are made (auth token handling), etc. Let me grep for relevant patterns.\n\nLet me first check the size and structure of app.js and style.css.",
