      {
        "type": "reasoning_text",
        "text": "Now I know the aroundIndex endpoint response shape: `{ conversationId, total, aroundIndex, radius, from, to, messages: [{role, text, at, index, id?}] }`.\n\nAnd the search endpoint (new) returns `{ hits: [{conversationId, title, at, snippet}] }`.\n\nNow there's a subtlety: the search hit doesn't include an `aroundIndex` (the message index of the hit). The snippet is from a transcript line, but `SessionSearchHit` has `conversationId, title, at, snippet` — NO message index. To use `aroundIndex`, the UI needs the message index of the hit. Hmm. The task §2.1 says \"结果列表 → 点一条 → 用 `aroundIndex` 拉到上下文 → 弹窗展示\". \n\nBut the search hits don't carry a message index! So how does the UI know which `aroundIndex` to use? \n\nHmm. Let me reconsider. The `SessionSearchHit` has `at` (epoch ms of the message). The aroundIndex endpoint uses message index (0-based position in transcript), NOT timestamp. So I need a way to map the hit to an index.\n\nOptions:\n1. Add an `index` field to `SessionSearchHit` — the message's index within its conversation's transcript. Then the search endpoint returns it, and the UI uses it as `aroundIndex`. But that requires modifying `session-search.ts` `searchTranscriptLines` and `SessionSearchHit` — which is allowed (not forbidden). But it changes the `recall_sessions` tool output shape too (adds a field). Is that OK? \"纯增量\" — adding a field to the tool output. Hmm, but the tool result is consumed by the model; adding a field might be fine. But it could be considered a contract change. Actually `SessionSearchHit` is an internal TS interface, not a `shared` schema. Adding an optional `index?` field is pure increment. But the tool's output goes to the model... let me check how the tool formats hits. Let me look at tools.ts around line 1121-1160.\n\nActually — wait. Let me reconsider. The `recall_sessions` tool returns hits with `conversationId, title, at, snippet`. To support \"定点找回\" (point-lookup), the UI needs a message index. But the tool was designed to give the model a `snippet` and `conversationId`, and the model then uses `recall` or the context endpoint with `aroundIndex`. But the tool output doesn't give an index either. Hmm — so how does the MODEL know the aroundIndex? Let me check the tool description and how it's used. Let me read tools.ts around 1121.\n\nActually — maybe the intended flow for the UI is different. Let me re-read §2.1: \"输入关键词 → 调 recall_sessions 的等价 HTTP（GET /api/v1/agent/sessions/search?q=&days=&limit=）; 结果列表 → 点一条 → 用 aroundIndex 拉到上下文 → 弹窗展示（只读，不 fork）\". \n\nSo clicking a result pulls context via aroundIndex. But the result must carry an index. Since `SessionSearchHit` doesn't have one, I have two choices:\n(a) Extend `SessionSearchHit` with an `index` (the message's absolute index in the transcript), returned by the new search endpoint. The `recall_sessions` tool also gets it (pure increment).\n(b) Have the UI call the context endpoint WITHOUT aroundIndex — but that returns `{used, window}` (context usage), not messages. So no.\n\nHmm, wait — actually, maybe the UI can call the context endpoint with `aroundIndex` derived differently. Let me reconsider: maybe the UI, upon clicking a hit, first fetches the FULL messages of that conversation (via `/messages`), finds the message matching the snippet/`at`, computes its index, then calls the context endpoint with that index. That's convoluted.\n\nActually, the cleanest: extend `SessionSearchHit` with an `index` field. Let me check whether that's safe. Let me look at how `searchTranscriptLines` builds hits and how the tool formats them. Actually, `searchTranscriptLines` iterates `source.lines` with a `for...of` loop — I can add an index via `source.lines.indexOf(line)` or better, enumerate. But the `lines` are built in `searchSessions` from `view.entries`, filtering to user/assistant messages. The \"index\" in the aroundIndex endpoint is the index into `readTranscript(chat, 0)` — the transcript message array. Are these the same? Let me check `readTranscript`. \n\nHmm — `searchSessions` builds `lines` from `conversation.context(CTX).entries`, filtering `pi.user`/`pi.assistant` entries. The aroundIndex endpoint uses `readTranscript(chat, 0)` which returns `TranscriptMessage[]`. Are these the same set/order? Let me check `readTranscript` in session.ts. These might differ (e.g., transcript might include tool messages, or might be structured differently). If they differ, the `index` from searchSessions' lines won't align with the aroundIndex index. That would be a bug.\n\nLet me check `readTranscript` and how `view.entries` compares. Let me read session.ts around readTranscript."
      }
