        "type": "reasoning_text",
        "text": "So `readTranscript` iterates `view.entries` and produces `messages` (one `TranscriptMessage` per user/assistant message with content). The `searchSessions` also iterates `view.entries` and produces `lines` (one `TranscriptLine` per user/assistant with text). \n\nAre the indices aligned? Let me think:\n- `readTranscript`: for each `pi.user` entry → pushes a user message (if text or images). For `pi.assistant` entries with text → pushes assistant message. It also handles tool steps but those don't add messages (they attach to a later assistant message). The `messages` array is roughly \"one entry per user/assistant message\".\n- `searchSessions.lines`: for each `pi.user`/`pi.assistant` entry with text → pushes a line.\n\nThere could be subtle differences: `readTranscript` skips user messages with NO text AND no images (line 1196), but `searchSessions` skips lines with empty text. A user message with images but no text: readTranscript includes it (images.length > 0), searchSessions skips it (text empty). So indices could diverge by such messages. Also, `readTranscript` might merge multiple assistant entries? Let me not go down this rabbit hole. The indices are NOT guaranteed to align.\n\nSo I should NOT assume the search hit's position in `lines` equals the `aroundIndex` in `readTranscript`. \n\nHmm. So how does the UI get a valid `aroundIndex` from a search hit? \n\nLet me reconsider. Maybe the design is: the new `sessions/search` endpoint should return an `index` that IS the aroundIndex-compatible index. To get that, the search would need to use `readTranscript`-equivalent indexing. But `searchSessions` uses its own `lines` from `view.entries`.\n\nHmm. Actually — wait. Let me reconsider the whole §2 flow. Maybe the UI doesn't need the exact hit index. Let me re-read §2.1: \"结果列表 → 点一条 → 用 `aroundIndex` 拉到上下文 → 弹窗展示（只读，不 fork）\". \n\nMaybe the intent: clicking a result opens the context viewer centered on the hit. But since the hit has `at` (timestamp), and the context endpoint is index-based... \n\nAlternatively — maybe the simplest correct approach: the search endpoint returns hits, and clicking a hit calls the context endpoint with `aroundIndex` derived from matching the hit to the conversation's messages. But that requires the UI to fetch messages and find the index by timestamp/text.\n\nHold on — let me reconsider. Maybe there's a cleaner approach: I can make the new `sessions/search` endpoint compute a CORRECT `aroundIndex` for each hit by using the SAME indexing as `readTranscript`. But that's complex and couples to session.ts (which imports lifecycle.ts → forbidden to touch, but importing is fine... actually session.ts imports lifecycle.ts; if I import session.ts into index.ts, that's already done indirectly. But using `readTranscript` from the search endpoint would require a `ResolvedChat` which needs `openChat`...).\n\nActually — hmm, let me step back. Let me reconsider whether the task really requires the hit result to carry an index. Re-reading: \"结果列表 → 点一条 → 用 aroundIndex 拉到上下文\". \n\nMaybe the simplest interpretation that WORKS: the search endpoint returns hits, each hit carrying enough to locate the message. Since `SessionSearchHit` has `at` (timestamp), the UI could... no.\n\nActually — you know what, let me reconsider. Let me check: does `searchSessions` have access to the line index? Yes — in `searchTranscriptLines`, the loop is `for (const source of sources) { for (const line of source.lines) {...} }`. I could add the line's index to the hit. And `source.lines` is built from `view.entries` filtered to user/assistant text messages. \n\nNow, is the `source.lines` index aligned with `readTranscript`'s `messages` index? Let me think again more carefully. Both derive from `view.entries`:\n- `readTranscript` produces `messages[]`: one per \"logical message\" = user message (with text or images) OR an assistant message with text (the final answer text; tool-call-only assistant entries produce steps, not messages). Actually wait — does readTranscript produce an assistant message for EACH `pi.assistant` entry, or only the final one? Let me read more of readTranscript.\n\nActually, this is getting really deep. Let me take a pragmatic alternative that sidesteps the index-alignment problem entirely:\n\n**The UI flow**: clicking a search result calls `GET /api/v1/agent/chat/:id/context?aroundIndex=0&radius=...`? No, that centers on message 0, not the hit.\n\nHmm. Let me reconsider — maybe the intended design is that the search endpoint returns an `index` field, and that index is defined as \"the index into the transcript as read by readTranscript\", and I make `searchSessions` compute it correctly by aligning with readTranscript's logic. But that's a lot of coupling and risk.\n\nWAIT. Let me re-read the task once more, very literally: \"结果列表 → 点一条 → 用 `aroundIndex` 拉到上下文 → 弹窗展示（只读，不 fork）\". \n\nMaybe the design is simpler than I think: the search hit includes `conversationId` and `at`. The UI, on click, fetches the conversation's messages (there's likely a `/messages` endpoint) and finds the index of the message whose `at` matches (or whose text contains the snippet), THEN calls the context endpoint with that `aroundIndex`. That's a reasonable UI flow and avoids backend changes to `SessionSearchHit`. But it's more complex in the frontend.\n\nOR — even simpler: maybe the search endpoint should return the `index` directly, computed by `searchSessions` from the `lines` array, AND the task's \"aroundIndex\" is understood to be the index into the search's own line array. But the context endpoint uses `readTranscript` indices. If they don't align, the clicked context would be slightly off (showing nearby but not exact messages). \n\nHmm. Let me actually VERIFY whether they align by reading the rest of `readTranscript`. If `readTranscript` produces exactly one message per `pi.user`/`pi.assistant` entry with text (skipping empty-text entries), then the indices WOULD align with `searchSessions.lines` (which also produces one line per `pi.user`/`pi.assistant` entry with text). Let me check.\n\nLet me read readTranscript lines 1198-1290."
