        "text": "Important: `readTranscript(chat, limit)` with `limit=50` (default) does `messages.slice(-limit)` — it returns the LAST 50 messages. But the aroundIndex endpoint calls `readTranscript(chat, 0)` with `limit=0` → returns ALL messages (no slicing). Good.\n\nNow comparing `readTranscript` messages vs `searchSessions` lines:\n- `readTranscript` pushes one message per `pi.user` entry (with text OR images) and one per `pi.assistant` entry with text (skipping tool-call-only assistant entries and empty-text). Plus a possible trailing \"pending steps\" assistant message with empty text.\n- `searchSessions.lines` pushes one line per `pi.user`/`pi.assistant` entry with text, INCLUDING tool-call-only assistant entries? Let me check: in searchSessions, for `pi.assistant` entries, it does `contentText(message.content).trim()` — for a tool-call-only assistant, `contentText` of toolCall parts... might be empty → skip. Actually `contentText` probably extracts text content; toolCall parts might yield empty → `.trim()` empty → skipped. So tool-call-only assistant entries are skipped in BOTH. \n\nBut there are differences:\n- A `pi.user` entry with images but NO text: `readTranscript` includes it (images > 0), `searchSessions` skips it (text empty). → divergence.\n- The trailing \"pending steps\" assistant (empty text): `readTranscript` includes it, `searchSessions` skips it (empty text). → divergence.\n\nSo indices CAN diverge. Therefore I cannot rely on `lines` index = `readTranscript` index.\n\nHmm. This is a real problem for \"点一条 → 用 aroundIndex 拉到上下文\". \n\nLet me think about the cleanest solution that's CORRECT:\n\nOption 1: The new search endpoint computes a correct `aroundIndex` by using the SAME `readTranscript` logic. To do that, the endpoint would need `openChat(conversationId)` → `ResolvedChat` → `readTranscript(chat, 0)`. Then find the message index whose text contains the query (or matches the hit's `at`). This is doable but couples the search endpoint to `readTranscript`/`openChat`. And `searchSessions` would need to be bypassed... but the task says \"搜索端点可复用它\" (reuse searchSessions). Hmm.\n\nOption 2: Make `searchSessions` (or a new variant) return an `index` that is the `readTranscript`-compatible index. But `searchSessions` uses `SessionReader` (a fake in tests), so it can't call `readTranscript`. Hmm.\n\nOption 3 (pragmatic UI-side): The UI, on clicking a hit, fetches the conversation messages via the existing `/messages` endpoint (which uses `readTranscript`), finds the index of the message matching the hit (by `at` timestamp or by text containing the snippet), and uses THAT index as `aroundIndex` for the context endpoint. This keeps the backend search endpoint simple (just `{ hits }`) and reuses `readTranscript`'s indices via `/messages`. But `/messages` returns max 50 by default? Let me check. Actually the context endpoint uses `readTranscript(chat, 0)` = all messages. The `/messages` endpoint — let me check its limit. Actually, this might be complex.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider — maybe I'm overcomplicating. Let me re-read the task's §2.1 ONE more time:\n\n\"输入关键词 → 调 `recall_sessions` 的等价 HTTP（若没有就加一个只读端点 `GET /api/v1/agent/sessions/search?q=&days=&limit=`）; 结果列表 → 点一条 → 用 `aroundIndex` 拉到上下文 → 弹窗展示（只读，不 fork）。\"\n\nMaybe \"用 aroundIndex 拉到上下文\" just means \"use the aroundIndex mechanism (the context endpoint) to pull context\". It doesn't necessarily mean the hit carries a precise index. Perhaps the UI calls the context endpoint with `aroundIndex=0` (or some default) and a large radius to show the conversation, and the user scrolls. But that's not \"定点找回\" (point-lookup).\n\nHmm. Actually, let me reconsider — maybe the intended flow is:\n1. Search returns hits, each with `at` (timestamp) and `conversationId`.\n2. Click hit → UI calls `GET .../context?aroundIndex=<computed>&radius=5`.\n3. To compute `aroundIndex`, the UI needs the index. \n\nThe cleanest way for the UI to get a correct index: the search endpoint returns an `index` for each hit, where `index` is the position in the FULL `readTranscript(chat, 0)` array. To produce this, the BACKEND search endpoint should compute it from `readTranscript`. \n\nActually — hold on. Let me reconsider using a DIFFERENT approach that's clean and reuses `searchSessions`: I can add an optional `index` to `SessionSearchHit` computed in `searchSessions` as the index of the line in `source.lines`. Then, to make it align with `readTranscript`, I'd change `searchSessions` to build `lines` using the SAME filtering as `readTranscript`... but I can't easily do that with the `SessionReader` abstraction (it yields `view.entries`, and readTranscript's logic is complex with steps).\n\nHmm, this is getting too deep. Let me step back and think about what's PRACTICAL and matches the task's intent without over-engineering.\n\nKEY REALIZATION: The task says \"只读不 fork\" and \"弹窗展示\" (show in a modal). The essential deliverable is: a UI where you search old conversations, click a result, and see the surrounding context in a read-only modal. The `aroundIndex` is the EXISTING backend mechanism (batch 20). \n\nI think the pragmatic, correct approach:\n- The new `sessions/search` endpoint returns hits, each including an `index` field = the message index (0-based) within that conversation, computed consistently with `readTranscript`. \n- To get this right, I'll have the search endpoint use `readTranscript`-based indexing. Actually — simplest: make `searchSessions` compute the index from `view.entries` in the SAME way `readTranscript` builds messages. But that duplicates complex logic.\n\nAlternatively — MUCH simpler and robust: Have the UI, upon clicking a hit, call the context endpoint with `aroundIndex` computed by the UI from a `/messages` fetch. Let me check if there's a `/messages` endpoint and its shape. Actually — hmm, the context endpoint response itself contains `messages` with `index` fields. So:\n\nUI flow:\n1. Search → hits (with `conversationId`, `at`, `snippet`, `title`).\n2. Click hit → call `GET /api/v1/agent/chat/:id/context?aroundIndex=0&radius=50` to get ALL messages (up to radius 50 → but a conversation might have more than 51 messages). Hmm, radius max is 50, so aroundIndex=0&radius=50 gives messages[0..50]. Not all. \n\nThat doesn't work for long conversations.\n\nOK, let me think differently. Let me just make the search endpoint return a correct `index` by computing it from the transcript via a NEW helper that reuses `readTranscript`. Actually — I realize the search endpoint can, for each hit, get the conversation via `openChat` and call `readTranscript(chat, 0)` to find the index of the matching message. But that's expensive (re-reading each conversation). But `searchSessions` ALREADY reads each conversation's context. So the marginal cost is low.\n\nHmm, but `searchSessions` takes a `SessionReader` and reads `view.entries` directly. I'd need to also call `readTranscript`, which needs a `ResolvedChat` (from `openChat`). \n\nLet me reconsider the architecture. Actually — maybe the cleanest is to NOT reuse `searchSessions`'s line-building but instead have the new endpoint compute indices using `readTranscript` directly, while still \"reusing\" the `searchTranscriptLines` pure core and the `days/limit/snippet` logic. Wait, but the task says \"搜索端点可复用它\" (can reuse searchSessions), which is a hint, not a mandate. \"若没有就加一个只读端点\" — add a read-only endpoint. The hint suggests reusing `session-search.ts`'s logic. \n\nHmm, let me reconsider — actually, maybe I'm overthinking the index alignment. Let me reconsider: is it acceptable for the clicked context to be \"approximately\" centered? No — \"定点找回\" means precise point-lookup. But honestly, for a UI feature, showing the surrounding messages near (but not exactly at) the hit might be acceptable IF the index is roughly right. But with image-only user messages and trailing steps messages, indices could be off by 1-2. That's a minor offset. Actually — the snippet search hit corresponds to a specific message. If the index is off by 1, the context window would still INCLUDE the hit message (since radius=5 gives ±5). So an off-by-few index would still show the hit in the window. That's actually FINE for a UI \"see the context around this old message\" feature! The window is ±5, so even if the index is off by 1-2, the relevant messages are shown.\n\nSo: adding an `index` to `SessionSearchHit` = the index of the line in `source.lines` (from `view.entries` user/assistant text messages) would be \"close enough\" to the `readTranscript` index — within the radius window. But it's not guaranteed. Hmm. Actually, for the modal to \"center\" on the hit, being off by a couple is acceptable since radius 5 covers it.\n\nBut wait — there's a cleaner, more robust option: **have the UI compute the exact index from the hit's `at` timestamp** by fetching the context endpoint is index-based... no.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider from a different angle: what if the `sessions/search` endpoint returns hits WITH `index`, where `index` is computed to be the `readTranscript`-compatible index by using `readTranscript`. Let me just DO that robustly. Here's a clean design:\n\nActually — the SIMPLEST robust approach: don't make searchSessions return an index at all. Instead, the new search endpoint returns hits as-is (`{conversationId, title, at, snippet}`), and the UI, on clicking a hit, calls `GET /api/v1/agent/chat/:id/messages` (the existing messages endpoint, which returns the full transcript with the SAME indexing as readTranscript) to find the index of the message with matching `at`/text, then calls the context endpoint with that `aroundIndex`. \n\nBut that requires knowing the `/messages` endpoint shape and limit. Let me check. Actually, let me check if there's a `/messages` endpoint and its default limit.\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Let me take yet another step back.\n\nActually — I bet the intended design is simpler than all this. Let me re-read: \"用 `aroundIndex` 拉到上下文\". Maybe the search endpoint is supposed to return hits where each hit has an `aroundIndex` (or `index`) field, and the task author assumed `recall_sessions` results have message indices. But they DON'T (as I found). \n\nGiven that, the cleanest thing that makes the feature WORK end-to-end: **add an `index` to `SessionSearchHit`** and have the new search endpoint return it. Make the `index` correct by computing it from `readTranscript`-compatible indexing. \n\nLet me figure out how to compute a correct index with minimal coupling. Idea: In `searchSessions`, when building `lines` from `view.entries`, I can ALSO compute the `readTranscript`-compatible index by replicating readTranscript's message-counting. But that's fragile.\n\nALTERNATIVE cleaner idea: Have the new `sessions/search` endpoint NOT use `searchSessions` line-building for indices, but instead, for each hit, find the index by calling `readTranscript`. Actually — even simpler: I can make `searchSessions` itself produce the `readTranscript`-compatible index by building `lines` from `readTranscript`-equivalent logic. But `readTranscript` takes a `ResolvedChat`, and `searchSessions` has a `Conversation` (from `reader.conversation`). Hmm — `readTranscript(chat)` needs `chat.externalId` for `messageIdOf` and `chat.conversation`. \n\nHold on. Let me reconsider — maybe the index alignment IS actually fine for the common case, and I'm overthinking. Let me check: for a NORMAL conversation (text-only user/assistant messages, no tool-call-only assistant entries producing steps, no image-only user messages, no trailing pending steps):\n- `readTranscript` messages = [user, assistant, user, assistant, ...] (one per text-bearing user/assistant entry).\n- `searchSessions.lines` = [user, assistant, user, assistant, ...] (one per text-bearing user/assistant entry).\n- These ARE aligned 1:1!\n\nThe divergences only occur in edge cases (image-only user messages, tool-call-only a