n    const { code, expiresAt, confirmToken } = store.begin();\n    assert.equal(typeof code, \"string\");\n    assert.ok(expiresAt > 0);\n    assert.ok(confirmToken.length >= 20, \"confirmToken 应是高熵随机串\");\n\n    const result = store.confirm(code, confirmToken, \"测试机\");\n    assert.notEqual(typeof result, \"string\", \"两段式都交齐应成功\");\n    const redeemed = result as RedeemResult;\n    assert.equal(store.verifyDeviceToken(redeemed.deviceToken), true);\n\n    // 落盘只有 sha256：明文 code 与 confirmToken 都不在文件里\n    const raw = readFileSync(file, \"utf8\");\n    assert.ok(!raw.includes(code), \"明文 code 不落盘\");\n    assert.ok(!raw.includes(confirmToken), \"明文 confirmToken 不落盘\");\n    assert.ok(!(\"confirmToken\" in (JSON.parse(raw) as {code?: unknown}).code!), \"字段名也不该是明文\");\n  } finally {\n    rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true });\n  }\n});\n```\n\nHmm — the last assertion is weird. Let me simplify: just check `!raw.includes(confirmToken)` and `!raw.includes(code)`. And maybe check that `code.hash` and `code.confirmHash` exist. Let me read raw JSON: `const stored = JSON.parse(raw) as { code?: { hash?: string; confirmHash?: string } }; assert.ok(stored.code?.hash); assert.ok(stored.code?.confirmHash);`. Good.\n\nTest 2 (only code):\n```ts\ntest(\"只扫码不能完成：confirm 只给 code（无 confirmToken）→ 拒绝、不发凭据\", () => {\n  const dir = ...;\n  const store = new PairingStore(file);\n  const { code } = store.begin();\n  const r1 = store.confirm(code, \"\", \"测试机\");\n  assert.equal(r1, \"confirm_required\");\n  // 也不能绕到单段 redeem 用这个 code 完成（两段式码带 confirmHash，redeem 路径不由本端点提供；\n  // 但至少确认此刻没产生任何设备）\n  assert.equal(store.listDevices().length, 0);\n  // confirm 没拿到 confirmToken 时计入 attempts：后续累计会撞上限\n});\n```\nHmm — actually, wait. I decided NOT to add the store-level `redeem` guard. So `store.redeem(code)` on a two-step code WOULD succeed. But at the HTTP level, `redeem` is gated. Hmm — should I test that a two-step code can't be redeemed single-stage? The \"只有 code 不能完成\" test could in