        "type": "reasoning_text",
        "text": "Important: `openChat(externalId)` with a NON-empty externalId does NOT create (it goes to the `else` branch starting line 354). But with an empty/undefined externalId, it CREATES a conversation. In my endpoint, `hit.conversationId` comes from `searchSessions` which iterates `chats.byExternal` keys — these are always non-empty strings. So `openChat(hit.conversationId)` with a non-empty id → read-only lookup. Good.\n\nBut — there's a subtle risk: `openChat` returns `null` if `!config` (no LLM config). In the http probe, `LLM_PROVIDER=faux` and there IS a config. But generally, if `readLlmConfig()` is null, `openChat` returns null → `messages = []` → `findAroundIndex` returns 0. That's fine (no crash).\n\nHmm — but actually, wait: `openChat` calls `getHarness()` internally too. And `readTranscript` re-reads. So for each hit, my endpoint calls `getHarness()` (via openChat) + `readTranscript`. Plus the initial `getHarness()` for searchSessions. That's fine.\n\nActually — hold on, let me reconsider: `openChat` requires `readLlmConfig()` to be non-null. If there's no LLM config (e.g., pure mock with no provider), `openChat` returns null → `messages=[]` → aroundIndex=0. The UI would then call the context endpoint which ALSO checks `readLlmConfig()` and returns 503. So the feature degrades gracefully. Fine.\n\nBut — actually, there's a concern for the HTTP PROBE: with `LLM_PROVIDER=faux`, is `readLlmConfig()` non-null? The context endpoint's aroundIndex path checks `if (!readLlmConfig()) return c.json({ ok: false, reason: \"llm_not_configured\" }, 503)`. So in the probe, if faux is configured, `readLlmConfig()` is non-null. Let me not worry — my endpoint doesn't call `readLlmConfig()` directly; it just gets `messages=[]` if openChat returns null. And I won't add an http probe test that requires real sessions (hard to set up). Let me focus the http probe §31 on the PAIRING endpoints (which don't need LLM) and a basic sessions/search shape check (empty hits → 200 `{hits:[]}`, empty q → 400). Actually — `searchSessions(await getHarness(), ...)` with no sessions → returns `[]`. That works even without LLM config? `getHarness()` opens durable — should work. Let me verify in the probe later. Actually — the `getHarness()` might need a valid model config to open... Let me check `Harness.open` in lifecycle.ts (line 1118). Hmm — I can't read lifecycle.ts? No, I CAN read it (just not EDIT it). Let me check quickly.\n\nActually — the instructions say \"另一个会话正在改 agent/injection.ts 与 agent/lifecycle.ts，你绝对不要碰这两个文件\". \"碰\" means touch/edit. Reading is fine (I already read session.ts which imports lifecycle). But to be safe, let me avoid unnecessary reads of lifecycle.ts. Actually — reading is definitely fine; \"碰\" in context = don't MODIFY (another session is editing them; I shouldn't create conflicts). Reading doesn't create conflicts. But let me minimize. \n\nLet me just check: `searchSessions` is already wired into `recall_sessions` via `getHarness()` in lifecycle.ts:477, and that tool works in the running server. So `getHarness()` works. Good. My endpoint uses the same path. Fine.\n\nActually — one concern: does `getHarness()` throw if there's no model config at all? Let me think — `Harness.open(...)` is called with config. Let me check line 1118 quickly to be safe. Actually — let me just trust it; the `recall_sessions` tool uses the exact same `getHarness().then(h => searchSessions(h, ...))`, so it's proven. If `getHarness()` can throw, the tool would too (and it has a try/catch). My endpoint doesn't have a try/catch around `getHarness()`. Hmm — should I add one? The task doesn't require error handling for this. But `app.onError` catches unhandled errors → 500. That's acceptable. Actually — to be safe, let me wrap in try/catch? No — over-engineering. The `app.onError` handles it. And `searchSessions` itself catches per-conversation errors. Fine.\n\nOK. Now — let me reconsider: the `findAroundIndex` + `openChat`/`readTranscript` per hit. Actually — I realize there might be a simpler concern: `readTranscript(chat, 0)` with `limit=0` returns ALL messages (no slice). For a conversation with thousands of messages, that's heavy. But it's the same as the context endpoint. Fine.\n\nOK. Now let me write the §2.4 unit tests in session-search.test.ts. I need to import the new helpers. Tests:\n1. \"搜索端点形状 / 空查询 400\": `parseSessionSearchQuery` → null for empty/short; valid → `{query, options}` shape; days/limit clamping.\n2. \"结果不含正文超长截断\": `searchSessions` with a fake reader producing a long line → hits shape + snippet ≤ 200 + no full body. Also `findAroundIndex` computes correct index.\n3. `sessionSearchApiEnabled` switch.\n4. `findAroundIndex` correctness (exact match by at+needle, fallback).\n\nLet me write them. Need to import `parseSessionSearchQuery`, `findAroundIndex`, `sessionSearchApiEnabled`.\n\nLet me design the \"shape\" test:\n```ts\ntest(\"搜索端点：parseSessionSearchQuery 形状 / 空查询 400 / days·limit 收敛\", () => {\n  assert.deepEqual(parseSessionSearchQuery({ q: \"  \" }), null, \"空查询 → null（调用方 400）\");\n  assert.equal(parseSessionSearchQuery({ q: \"爬\" }), null, \"短于 2 字 → null\");\n  const parsed = parseSessionSearchQuery({ q: \"  爬山  \", days: \"60\", limit: \"3\" });\n  assert.deepEqual(parsed, { query: \"爬山\", options: { days: 60, limit: 3 } });\n  // 非法 / 缺省 days·limit 不进 options，交给检索核心回落\n  assert.deepEqual(parseSessionSearchQuery({ q: \"爬山\", days: \"abc\", limit: \"\" }), {\n    query: \"爬山\", options: { limit: 0 },\n  });\n});\n```\nHmm — `limit: \"\"` → `Number(\"\")` = 0 → finite → `options.limit = 0`. Then `clampInt(0, 5, 1, 20)` → 1. Hmm. Let me reconsider — should `limit: \"\"` be treated as \"not provided\"? `raw.limit === undefined` check — `\"\"` is not undefined, so `Number(\"\")` = 0 → included. That's a bit odd. Let me handle: if the raw string is empty/whitespace, treat as undefined. Let me refine `parseSessionSearchQuery`:\n```ts\nconst daysRaw = raw.days?.trim();\nconst limitRaw = raw.limit?.trim();\nconst days = daysRaw ? Number(daysRaw) : undefined;\nconst limit = limitRaw ? Number(limitRaw) : undefined;\n```\nThat's cleaner. Let me update the function. Actually — let me reconsider: `Number(\"12abc\")` = NaN → not finite → skipped. `Number(\" 12 \")` → after trim \"12\" → 12. Good. Let me update.\n\nLet me edit `parseSessionSearchQuery` to trim days/limit."
      }
