    "rawContent": [
      {
        "type": "reasoning_text",
        "text": "`esc(text)` calls `text.replace(...)` — so `text` must be a string. If `text` is undefined/null, it'll throw. I need to be careful: `esc(hit.title || \"（无标题）\")` — hit.title is a string (from searchSessions title, always a string). `esc(hit.snippet)` — string. `esc(new Date(hit.at).toLocaleString(\"zh-CN\"))` — string. `esc(msg.text || \"（空）\")` — string. `esc(error.message)` — string. Good. But to be safe, let me wrap with `String(...)`. Actually — `esc` is used elsewhere with `esc(hit.hint)` where hint is a string. Let me use `esc(String(x ?? \"\"))` for safety on dynamic values. Actually — the existing `wireSettingsSearch` uses `esc(hit.hint)` directly. Let me follow the pattern but add `String()` for robustness. Actually — let me define a local helper `safe = (v) => esc(String(v ?? \"\"))` inside openRecallModal. Hmm — or just use `esc(String(...))` inline. Let me keep it clean.\n\nAlso — `esc()` doesn't escape quotes. For text content (not attributes), that's fine. For attribute values, use `attr()`. I'll avoid putting user text in attributes (use textContent or esc'd innerHTML). Actually — I'm using `row.innerHTML = ...${esc(hit.title)}...` — text content, so esc (which escapes &<>) is enough. Quotes in text content are harmless. Good.\n\nNow let me look at the modal CSS to design the recall styles consistently."
      }
    ],
