        "text": "Interesting: `app.js` first line shows `^M$` (CRLF), but `grep -c $'\\r' app.js` returned 0... That's odd. Wait — the output shows:\n```\napp.js:\n/**^M$\nstyle.css:\n/*^M$\napp.js CRLF count:\n0\n```\nThen exit code 1 (because `grep -c` for style.css returned... hmm, the command chained with `&&`, and `grep -c $'\\r' app.js` returned 0 matches → grep exits 1 → the `&&` chain stopped, so `style.css CRLF count` wasn't printed). \n\nWait — `head -1 app.js | cat -A` shows `/**^M$` meaning there IS a `\\r`. But `grep -c $'\\r' app.js` returned 0. That's contradictory. Oh — `$'\\r'` in Git Bash might not be interpreted as CR correctly, OR the file has mixed line endings. Hmm.\n\nActually — wait. `cat -A` shows `^M` for CR. The first line `/**^M$` has a CR. But grep for `\\r` found 0? Let me reconsider — maybe `head -1` reads the first LINE but the file's line endings are LF, and the `^M` is from... no, `cat -A` would show `$` for LF-only. `^M$` means CRLF.\n\nHmm — but grep -c $'\\r' returned 0. Maybe `$'\\r'` wasn't passed correctly through the tool. Let me test differently. Actually — this is getting into the weeds. The important thing: my `Edit` calls to app.js SUCCEEDED, meaning the old_string matched. If app.js were CRLF, my old_string (with `\\n`) wouldn't match unless the Edit tool normalizes. Since the edits succeeded, either (a) app.js is actually LF and cat -A showed something else, or (b) the Edit tool normalizes line endings when matching.\n\nActually — wait. Let me reconsider: the earlier `Read` tool output for app.js showed lines WITHOUT visible `\\r`. And my edits succeeded. So the Edit tool must be handling it. Hmm — but actually, maybe the file is CRLF and the Edit tool reads it and my old_string with `\\n` matched because... no.\n\nLet me just check more carefully with a direct test. Actually — let me not overthink. The critical question: will my app.js edits have consistent line endings, and will `node --check` pass? Let me just run `node --check` to verify syntax. And for style.css, I'll append via a method that respects CRLF. \n\nActually — the safest approach for appending to style.css: use the Edit tool with an anchor that's the last line. But if style.css is CRLF, my new_string with `\\n` would introduce LF lines into a CRLF file (mixed endings) — cosmetically ugly but functionally fine (CSS doesn't care). But it could look bad in a diff. Hmm.\n\nLet me determine the line endings definitively. Let me use `file` or a node one-liner."
      }
