verall diff is sane. Let me also re-verify `node --check` once more and check line endings are still clean (no mixed endings introduced).\n\nActually — let me also reconsider one potential issue I glossed over: in `auxiliaryModel`, the module-level `warnedNoCallSite` set means the warn fires only ONCE per process for `title` and once for `vision`. But in production, `auxiliaryModel(\"title\")` is NEVER called (no call site). So the warn would never fire in production! That's the whole point — \"避免有人配了以为生效\" — but if there's no call site, `auxiliaryModel(\"title\")` is never invoked, so the warn never prints. Hmm. That's a logical gap: the warn only fires when someone CALLS `auxiliaryModel(\"title\")`, but there's no production caller. So configuring `AUX_MODEL_TITLE` and never seeing a warn is still \"配了以为生效\" silently.\n\nHmm — well, the task says \"auxiliaryModel(\\\"title\\\") / (\\\"vision\\\") 调用时打一条 warn\" — i.e., the warn fires WHEN CALLED. Since there's no production call site, it won't fire in normal operation. But that's what the task asked for literally: \"调用时打一条 warn\". The warn is a tripwire: if someone LATER adds a call site (or tests it), they'll see the warn. And §3.3's test calls it directly and asserts the warn. So the implementation matches the task literally. The \"avoid configured-but-thought-effective\" benefit is somewhat theoretical (it fires on any call, including a test or a future call site). \n\nActually — hmm, wait. Could I make the warn fire at CONFIG time? E.g., when someone sets `AUX_MODEL_TITLE`, warn at startup? That would require scanning env at startup, which is more invasive and not what the task asked. The task explicitly says \"调用时打一条 warn\" (when called). So my implementation is correct per the literal spec. I'll note this nuance in the change record? Actually — I already documented \"配了照常返回配置值，但没人会拿它发模型调用\". Good enough. Let me not over-engineer.\n\nActually — let me reconsider: maybe I should ALSO have `auxiliaryModel` warn even when there's no `current` model... no, I placed it at the top, so it fires on every call regardless. Good.\n\nHmm — but actually, there's a subtle concern: the existing test `\"pin 只在别的供应商里有 → 用那个供应商\"` calls `auxiliaryModel(\"title\", ...)` and triggers a `console.warn`. In the FULL test suite output, that warn line appears. It's harmless. But let me reconsider whether to suppress it to keep output clean. Actually — I'll leave it; it's fine and demonstrates the feature.\n\nWait — actually, let me reconsider a REAL issue: the module-level `warnedNoCallSite` set is shared across the WHOLE test process. In `pnpm --filter @lifediary/server test`, all test files run in the same process? If so, and if some OTHER test file calls `auxiliaryModel(\"title\")` (none do, based on grep), it's fine. And my `auxiliary.test.ts` tests inject their own `warned` sets for the §3.3 tests. But — the EXISTING test `\"pin 只在别的供应商里有\"` does NOT inject a `warned` set, so it uses the module set and consumes `title`'s \"once\". That's fine (no assertion). OK.\n\nLet me now also double-check: did I break the `AUX_USES` usage anywhere else? I grepped earlier: only `auxiliary.ts` (definition) and `auxiliary.test.ts` (assertion). But let me also check if any PROVIDERS probe or other script iterates `AUX_USES` at runtime and would now hit `vision`. Let me grep the whole ts tree for `AUX_USES` once more to be safe (including scripts).\n\nEarlier grep with `grep -v \"\\.test\\.ts\"` and `grep -v node_modules` showed only auxiliary.ts and auxiliary.test.ts. But I excluded test files. Let me check scripts too. Actually the grep was over `ts --include=\"*.ts\"` which includes scripts. It showed only auxiliary.ts (def) + auxiliary.test.ts (assertion). So no other consumer. Good.\n\nNow — let me also verify: the `auxPinnedModelId` uses `AUX_MODEL_${use.toUpperCase()}`. For `vision` → `AUX_MODEL_VISION`. Good. No call site, inert. Fine.\n\nLet me do a final sanity check: git diff stat + confirm no forbidden files + node --check + line endings."
